Feed Windows touch and pen into GPUI's touches, from `WM_POINTER` messages, and drop the mouse copies.

What: gpui_windows handles `WM_POINTERDOWN`, `WM_POINTERUPDATE` and `WM_POINTERUP` for touch (`PT_TOUCH`) and pen
(`PT_PEN`) pointers. The handling is ported from winit v0.30.13 (latest stable release,
e9809ef54b18499bb4f2cac945719ecc2a61061b), `src/platform_impl/windows/event_loop.rs`. For each message, every pointer
in its frame and the frame's coalesced history are read (`GetPointerFrameInfoHistory`, oldest first), then the frame's
other messages are skipped (`SkipPointerFrameMessages`). Positions are sub-pixel, from the HIMETRIC location mapped
through `GetPointerDeviceRects`; when the digitizer has no rect, `ptPixelLocation` is used. Pressure comes from
`GetPointerTouchInfo` / `GetPointerPenInfo`, normalized as winit does (1..=1024 → `force`, otherwise `None`). Each
contact becomes a `TouchEvent` with:
- a `TouchId` of its own, never reused (Windows reuses pointer ids);
- a phase from a flags table: `POINTER_FLAG_CANCELED` → Cancelled, then DOWN → Started, UP → Ended, UPDATE in
  contact → Moved;
- `device` Touch or Pen;
- `time_stamp`: the pointer's `PerformanceCount` (else its `dwTime`) moved onto GPUI's clock by its age against
  `QueryPerformanceCounter` (else `GetTickCount`), as patch 0020 does on iOS and the web.
A pointer the window loses while down (`WM_POINTERCAPTURECHANGED`) is cancelled at its last position. A move, up or
cancel for a pointer that never went down in the window (it went down on the title bar or in another window) is
dropped, and so is a second down. A pen hovering in range (UPDATE without contact) is a mouse move, through the
existing `WM_MOUSEMOVE` handler; Flutter's embedder sends it as a pointer hover too. Client-area mouse messages that
Windows makes from touch or pen go to `DefWindowProc` without reaching GPUI: those whose `GetMessageExtraInfo`,
masked by 0xFFFFFF00, is `MI_WP_SIGNATURE` 0xFF515700 (Flutter 3.50.1's `GetFlutterPointerDeviceKind` check,
`flutter_window.cc`). A tap therefore reaches GPUI once, as a touch, and GPUI's recognizer synthesizes its click.
Non-client messages stay, so a touch on a title bar still moves the window. Mouse and touchpad pointers are untouched:
`EnableMouseInPointer` stays off, as in winit, so the mouse stays on `WM_MOUSE*`, and the touchpad stays on Direct
Manipulation. Pen barrel and eraser buttons, tilt and rotation are not carried (`TouchEvent` has no field for them).
The decisions are in a new `pointer.rs` on plain values: the device table, the flags table, the contacts state
machine, the promoted-message table, the clock and the HIMETRIC mapping. Their table tests run on any host, and
`events.rs` asserts the WinUser.h values written out there against the `windows` crate.

Why: GPUI's Windows backend handled no touch at all. Windows promoted each touch to mouse messages, so a tap was a
click with `PointerDevice::Mouse`, a drag was a mouse drag (no pan, fling, long press or pinch), and pens had no
pressure. GPUI's touch pipeline (recognizers, pinch, overscroll) only runs on `PlatformInput::Touch`, which only a
backend can send, so neither a Parallax crate nor an extension trait can add this. Desktop touch is always compiled
in (`cfg(target_os = "windows")` is the crate's own); the behaviour follows the pointer type at runtime.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

